{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-787",
              "name": "Pre-authentication heap buffer overflow via unbounded appends to the per-client MQTT write buffer",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pre-authentication heap buffer overflow via unbounded appends to the per-client MQTT write buffer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The broker allocates each client's write buffer at exactly `max_request_size` bytes but no code path ever checks whether the next packet fits, so protocol messages larger than the remaining space are written past the end of the heap allocation. A single unauthenticated SUBSCRIBE that replays retained messages - or a session resume, or a QoS retransmit - overruns the buffer with bytes the attacker chose, and the shipped configuration crashes. The same unchecked append exists at twelve further write sites, so the buffer can also be overrun by ordinary protocol traffic once it is nearly full."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The broker allocates each client's write buffer at exactly `max_request_size` bytes but no code path ever checks whether the next packet fits, so protocol messages larger than the remaining space are written past the end of the heap allocation. A single unauthenticated SUBSCRIBE that replays retained messages - or a session resume, or a QoS retransmit - overruns the buffer with bytes the attacker chose, and the shipped configuration crashes. The same unchecked append exists at twelve further write sites, so the buffer can also be overrun by ordinary protocol traffic once it is nearly full.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer can write attacker-chosen bytes of attacker-chosen length beyond a heap allocation inside the broker process, and reliably terminates the service. Demonstrated consequences are heap corruption outside the `wbuf` object (the overflowed bytes were proven to be the attacker's own PUBLISH payload, landing at `wbuf+908` in a 1024-byte buffer) and process death: AddressSanitizer aborts, and the plain optimized build exits with SIGSEGV on the same input. Because the broker is the single broker in the deployment, every connected client loses its session and message flow, giving a trivial denial of service against all tenants. The write primitive also reaches adjacent heap objects and `write_data()` will transmit `towrite - wrote` bytes from `wbuf`, so out-of-bounds read/disclosure of neighbouring heap contents is reachable once the length tracker is corrupted; that read-back and control-flow hijack were not demonstrated and are not scored here.\n\nIntroduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe, which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`, and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully instead of silently dropping replies.",
                "markdown": "The broker allocates each client's write buffer at exactly `max_request_size` bytes but no code path ever checks whether the next packet fits, so protocol messages larger than the remaining space are written past the end of the heap allocation. A single unauthenticated SUBSCRIBE that replays retained messages - or a session resume, or a QoS retransmit - overruns the buffer with bytes the attacker chose, and the shipped configuration crashes. The same unchecked append exists at twelve further write sites, so the buffer can also be overrun by ordinary protocol traffic once it is nearly full.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer can write attacker-chosen bytes of attacker-chosen length beyond a heap allocation inside the broker process, and reliably terminates the service. Demonstrated consequences are heap corruption outside the `wbuf` object (the overflowed bytes were proven to be the attacker's own PUBLISH payload, landing at `wbuf+908` in a 1024-byte buffer) and process death: AddressSanitizer aborts, and the plain optimized build exits with SIGSEGV on the same input. Because the broker is the single broker in the deployment, every connected client loses its session and message flow, giving a trivial denial of service against all tenants. The write primitive also reaches adjacent heap objects and `write_data()` will transmit `towrite - wrote` bytes from `wbuf`, so out-of-bounds read/disclosure of neighbouring heap contents is reachable once the length tracker is corrupted; that read-back and control-flow hijack were not demonstrated and are not scored here.\n\nIntroduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe, which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`, and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully instead of silently dropping replies."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-787",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-416",
              "name": "Pre-authentication use-after-free of a shared MQTT session on ClientId reuse",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pre-authentication use-after-free of a shared MQTT session on ClientId reuse"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The handler that lets a second connection resume an existing MQTT session by ClientId does not take a reference on that session, while the connection that created it releases the only reference when it disconnects. The session object is therefore freed while the second connection is still connected and still using it, and that connection then reads and writes freed heap memory. Any unauthenticated peer can set this up with two connections and one disconnect."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The handler that lets a second connection resume an existing MQTT session by ClientId does not take a reference on that session, while the connection that created it releases the only reference when it disconnects. The session object is therefore freed while the second connection is still connected and still using it, and that connection then reads and writes freed heap memory. Any unauthenticated peer can set this up with two connections and one disconnect.\n\nA second unauthenticated connection can obtain a ClientId's session, be told by the broker that it resumed that session (`session_present=1`), and keep using a session object that the broker frees as soon as the first connection disconnects. The demonstrated consequence is that the surviving connection then operates on freed heap memory: it copies 64 bytes of freed session data into a new subscriber record and links that record into a subscription list that teardown already destroyed. Under AddressSanitizer this aborts the connection thread; on the plain optimized build it completes silently, leaving the connection reading and writing a 328-byte heap block that is now reusable by any other allocation in the process. Demonstrated integrity impact is limited to that corruption of freed memory, and no service interruption was observed on the production build; what would turn this into code execution is reclaiming the freed block with attacker-controlled bytes, which was attempted but not achieved because the two connections are served by different event-loop threads and the chunk was not handed back within the test window.\n\nGive each connection its own reference to the session it uses, and make teardown release exactly the references that were taken, so that no connection can be left holding a block whose count has reached zero. Establish an explicit ownership model first: the sessions map holds one reference while the session is registered, and every connection that points at the session holds one of its own. Under that model the creation path must take two references, the reuse path must take one for the resuming connection, and the clean-session discard path must release the map reference while clearing the local pointer. Teardown must then release the connection's reference on every path, including connections whose clean-session flag is false, and release the map reference only when the session is being discarded. Setting the client's session pointer to null as part of that release prevents a recycled client block from releasing a stale reference. Apply the same symmetry to the branch that discards an existing session for a clean-session connection, which currently removes the map entry without releasing its reference and therefore leaks the block on every repeat. Beyond the reference counting, consider binding session ownership to the connection that created it - for example refusing to hand an existing session to a second concurrent connection, or explicitly closing the previous connection when a ClientId is reused - so that two live connections cannot share mutable session state at all; MQTT permits a connection to take over a ClientId, but the broker should then invalidate the previous connection rather than leave it with a pointer into shared state. Finally, the session's subscription lists are destroyed during teardown while other handlers can still reach them; making the session's lifetime solely reference-counted, as above, is what removes that window.",
                "markdown": "The handler that lets a second connection resume an existing MQTT session by ClientId does not take a reference on that session, while the connection that created it releases the only reference when it disconnects. The session object is therefore freed while the second connection is still connected and still using it, and that connection then reads and writes freed heap memory. Any unauthenticated peer can set this up with two connections and one disconnect.\n\nA second unauthenticated connection can obtain a ClientId's session, be told by the broker that it resumed that session (`session_present=1`), and keep using a session object that the broker frees as soon as the first connection disconnects. The demonstrated consequence is that the surviving connection then operates on freed heap memory: it copies 64 bytes of freed session data into a new subscriber record and links that record into a subscription list that teardown already destroyed. Under AddressSanitizer this aborts the connection thread; on the plain optimized build it completes silently, leaving the connection reading and writing a 328-byte heap block that is now reusable by any other allocation in the process. Demonstrated integrity impact is limited to that corruption of freed memory, and no service interruption was observed on the production build; what would turn this into code execution is reclaiming the freed block with attacker-controlled bytes, which was attempted but not achieved because the two connections are served by different event-loop threads and the chunk was not handed back within the test window.\n\nGive each connection its own reference to the session it uses, and make teardown release exactly the references that were taken, so that no connection can be left holding a block whose count has reached zero. Establish an explicit ownership model first: the sessions map holds one reference while the session is registered, and every connection that points at the session holds one of its own. Under that model the creation path must take two references, the reuse path must take one for the resuming connection, and the clean-session discard path must release the map reference while clearing the local pointer. Teardown must then release the connection's reference on every path, including connections whose clean-session flag is false, and release the map reference only when the session is being discarded. Setting the client's session pointer to null as part of that release prevents a recycled client block from releasing a stale reference. Apply the same symmetry to the branch that discards an existing session for a clean-session connection, which currently removes the map entry without releasing its reference and therefore leaks the block on every repeat. Beyond the reference counting, consider binding session ownership to the connection that created it - for example refusing to hand an existing session to a second concurrent connection, or explicitly closing the previous connection when a ClientId is reused - so that two live connections cannot share mutable session state at all; MQTT permits a connection to take over a ClientId, but the broker should then invalidate the previous connection rather than leave it with a pointer into shared state. Finally, the session's subscription lists are destroyed during teardown while other handlers can still reach them; making the session's lifetime solely reference-counted, as above, is what removes that window."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-416",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/416.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-476",
              "name": "Pre-auth remote process kill via NULL dispatch on unimplemented MQTT packet types (`handlers[]` / `unpack_handlers[]`)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pre-auth remote process kill via NULL dispatch on unimplemented MQTT packet types (`handlers[]` / `unpack_handlers[]`)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any unauthenticated TCP peer can terminate the `sol` broker with a single two-byte MQTT packet. The broker's fixed-header opcode gate admits every type from CONNECT (1) to DISCONNECT (14), but the command dispatch table has NULL entries for the packet types it does not implement, and the dispatch entry point calls the table slot without a NULL check. Sending PINGRESP (`D0 00`) therefore performs an indirect call to address 0. A parallel defect exists in the decoder table, which has only 11 entries, so UNSUBACK (type 11) indexes out of bounds while CONNACK (2) and SUBACK (9) call NULL unpack handlers."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any unauthenticated TCP peer can terminate the `sol` broker with a single two-byte MQTT packet. The broker's fixed-header opcode gate admits every type from CONNECT (1) to DISCONNECT (14), but the command dispatch table has NULL entries for the packet types it does not implement, and the dispatch entry point calls the table slot without a NULL check. Sending PINGRESP (`D0 00`) therefore performs an indirect call to address 0. A parallel defect exists in the decoder table, which has only 11 entries, so UNSUBACK (type 11) indexes out of bounds while CONNACK (2) and SUBACK (9) call NULL unpack handlers.\n\n**Unauthenticated, zero-interaction availability loss for the whole broker process.** A single two-byte packet sent on a fresh TCP connection — no CONNECT, no credentials, no prior state — terminates the listening daemon (SIGSEGV). Every connected client is dropped, in-flight QoS state and the in-memory retained-message store are lost, and the broker stays down until something outside the process restarts it. The same death is reachable from four distinct packet types and through both dispatch tables, so filtering a single opcode does not mitigate it.\n\nNo confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed: the crash address is exactly `0x0` and the call target is not attacker-controlled, so no read/write primitive or control-flow influence was demonstrated. The demonstrated effect is process termination only (`C:N` / `I:N` / `A:H`).\n\nReject packet types the broker does not implement before they reach any dispatch table, and make both dispatch sites defensive. Concretely: introduce a single predicate that enumerates the packet types a client may send (CONNECT, PUBLISH, PUBACK, PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP, SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, PINGREQ, DISCONNECT) and call it from the framing gate so CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PINGRESP and the reserved opcodes are rejected with a protocol error and the connection closed. Size both the decoder and the command handler tables for the whole 4-bit opcode space (16 entries) with an explicit, commented slot per type so a missing entry is deliberate rather than accidental, and make both lookup sites bound the index and treat a NULL entry as a protocol error instead of calling through it. As defence in depth, consider having the decoder return an error that the caller acts on, rather than ignoring the decode result, so a future table gap cannot silently fall through to a handler that was never designed to run for that packet type.",
                "markdown": "Any unauthenticated TCP peer can terminate the `sol` broker with a single two-byte MQTT packet. The broker's fixed-header opcode gate admits every type from CONNECT (1) to DISCONNECT (14), but the command dispatch table has NULL entries for the packet types it does not implement, and the dispatch entry point calls the table slot without a NULL check. Sending PINGRESP (`D0 00`) therefore performs an indirect call to address 0. A parallel defect exists in the decoder table, which has only 11 entries, so UNSUBACK (type 11) indexes out of bounds while CONNACK (2) and SUBACK (9) call NULL unpack handlers.\n\n**Unauthenticated, zero-interaction availability loss for the whole broker process.** A single two-byte packet sent on a fresh TCP connection — no CONNECT, no credentials, no prior state — terminates the listening daemon (SIGSEGV). Every connected client is dropped, in-flight QoS state and the in-memory retained-message store are lost, and the broker stays down until something outside the process restarts it. The same death is reachable from four distinct packet types and through both dispatch tables, so filtering a single opcode does not mitigate it.\n\nNo confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed: the crash address is exactly `0x0` and the call target is not attacker-controlled, so no read/write primitive or control-flow influence was demonstrated. The demonstrated effect is process termination only (`C:N` / `I:N` / `A:H`).\n\nReject packet types the broker does not implement before they reach any dispatch table, and make both dispatch sites defensive. Concretely: introduce a single predicate that enumerates the packet types a client may send (CONNECT, PUBLISH, PUBACK, PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP, SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, PINGREQ, DISCONNECT) and call it from the framing gate so CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PINGRESP and the reserved opcodes are rejected with a protocol error and the connection closed. Size both the decoder and the command handler tables for the whole 4-bit opcode space (16 entries) with an explicit, commented slot per type so a missing entry is deliberate rather than accidental, and make both lookup sites bound the index and treat a NULL entry as a protocol error instead of calling through it. As defence in depth, consider having the decoder return an error that the caller acts on, rather than ignoring the decode result, so a future table gap cannot silently fall through to a handler that was never designed to run for that packet type."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-476",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-191",
              "name": "Non-terminating SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE tuple loops: unsigned underflow lets one 7-byte pre-auth packet crash or exhaust the broker",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Non-terminating SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE tuple loops: unsigned underflow lets one 7-byte pre-auth packet crash or exhaust the broker"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Both the SUBSCRIBE and the UNSUBSCRIBE decoders walk their payload in a loop whose counter is the sender-declared packet length, and they subtract the declared topic length from it before checking that those bytes are actually present. Declaring a topic longer than the packet wraps the counter to about 2^64, so the loop never terminates: it keeps reallocating and allocating memory and keeps copying from beyond the end of the connection's read buffer. A single unauthenticated 7-byte packet kills the shipped build with SIGSEGV in about 0.2 s; with a larger read buffer it instead grows resident memory by roughly 900 MB in half a second while pinning a worker thread at full CPU, and the process no longer honours SIGTERM."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Both the SUBSCRIBE and the UNSUBSCRIBE decoders walk their payload in a loop whose counter is the sender-declared packet length, and they subtract the declared topic length from it before checking that those bytes are actually present. Declaring a topic longer than the packet wraps the counter to about 2^64, so the loop never terminates: it keeps reallocating and allocating memory and keeps copying from beyond the end of the connection's read buffer. A single unauthenticated 7-byte packet kills the shipped build with SIGSEGV in about 0.2 s; with a larger read buffer it instead grows resident memory by roughly 900 MB in half a second while pinning a worker thread at full CPU, and the process no longer honours SIGTERM.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer needs one TCP connection and 7 bytes - no CONNECT, no credentials - to take the broker down. On a build with the project's production flags the whole process dies with SIGSEGV within a fraction of a second (denial of service for every connected client). With the shipped 512 KB / 1 MB read-buffer configuration, and on builds where the allocator keeps up, the same packet instead produces an unbounded loop: resident memory grew from 52 MB to 965 MB within 0.5 s while one of the two event-loop threads stayed at ~95 % CPU, the connection stayed open, no diagnostic was produced, and the process ignored SIGTERM. In that state roughly half of the broker's event-loop capacity is lost permanently per malicious connection, and further connections exhaust memory until the process is killed. The same loop copies topic bytes from beyond the end of the read buffer, so callers that do not crash first perform unbounded out-of-bounds heap reads.\n\n1. Validate the remaining length before every subtraction in both tuple loops: reject the packet when fewer than the 2-byte topic length plus the 1-byte QoS level (SUBSCRIBE) or fewer than the 2-byte topic length (UNSUBSCRIBE) remain, and reject it again when the declared topic length exceeds what is left after the topic length field has been consumed. Reject before reading the packet identifier when the declared length cannot hold it plus at least one complete tuple.\n2. Make the loop counter itself trustworthy: derive each iteration's bound from the bytes actually present in the packet rather than from the declared length alone, and fail closed on any packet whose contents do not exactly match its declared length.\n3. Remove the unreachable error branch or make the allocation helper return an error instead of terminating the process, so that a decode failure costs one connection rather than the whole broker.\n4. Reject and discard decoded packets that failed validation: the caller currently ignores the decoder's return code and dispatches the partially populated packet to the command handlers, which then operate on an empty tuple list.\n5. Bound the tuple count to the 16-bit field that stores it, and free the tuples already decoded when validation fails so that malformed packets cannot leak memory.\n6. Apply the same availability-before-subtraction rule to the remaining length arithmetic in the PUBLISH and CONNECT decoders, which use the same pattern.\n7. Add regression tests that feed each decoder truncated and length-inconsistent packets and assert a clean protocol error plus a live broker process.",
                "markdown": "Both the SUBSCRIBE and the UNSUBSCRIBE decoders walk their payload in a loop whose counter is the sender-declared packet length, and they subtract the declared topic length from it before checking that those bytes are actually present. Declaring a topic longer than the packet wraps the counter to about 2^64, so the loop never terminates: it keeps reallocating and allocating memory and keeps copying from beyond the end of the connection's read buffer. A single unauthenticated 7-byte packet kills the shipped build with SIGSEGV in about 0.2 s; with a larger read buffer it instead grows resident memory by roughly 900 MB in half a second while pinning a worker thread at full CPU, and the process no longer honours SIGTERM.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer needs one TCP connection and 7 bytes - no CONNECT, no credentials - to take the broker down. On a build with the project's production flags the whole process dies with SIGSEGV within a fraction of a second (denial of service for every connected client). With the shipped 512 KB / 1 MB read-buffer configuration, and on builds where the allocator keeps up, the same packet instead produces an unbounded loop: resident memory grew from 52 MB to 965 MB within 0.5 s while one of the two event-loop threads stayed at ~95 % CPU, the connection stayed open, no diagnostic was produced, and the process ignored SIGTERM. In that state roughly half of the broker's event-loop capacity is lost permanently per malicious connection, and further connections exhaust memory until the process is killed. The same loop copies topic bytes from beyond the end of the read buffer, so callers that do not crash first perform unbounded out-of-bounds heap reads.\n\n1. Validate the remaining length before every subtraction in both tuple loops: reject the packet when fewer than the 2-byte topic length plus the 1-byte QoS level (SUBSCRIBE) or fewer than the 2-byte topic length (UNSUBSCRIBE) remain, and reject it again when the declared topic length exceeds what is left after the topic length field has been consumed. Reject before reading the packet identifier when the declared length cannot hold it plus at least one complete tuple.\n2. Make the loop counter itself trustworthy: derive each iteration's bound from the bytes actually present in the packet rather than from the declared length alone, and fail closed on any packet whose contents do not exactly match its declared length.\n3. Remove the unreachable error branch or make the allocation helper return an error instead of terminating the process, so that a decode failure costs one connection rather than the whole broker.\n4. Reject and discard decoded packets that failed validation: the caller currently ignores the decoder's return code and dispatches the partially populated packet to the command handlers, which then operate on an empty tuple list.\n5. Bound the tuple count to the 16-bit field that stores it, and free the tuples already decoded when validation fails so that malformed packets cannot leak memory.\n6. Apply the same availability-before-subtraction rule to the remaining length arithmetic in the PUBLISH and CONNECT decoders, which use the same pattern.\n7. Add regression tests that feed each decoder truncated and length-inconsistent packets and assert a clean protocol error plus a live broker process."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-191",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/191.html"
            },
