                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "/*\n                 * The header carries the ack type, it used to be left\n                 * uninitialized and the retransmit packed the wrong packet\n                 * while sizing this one.\n                 */\n                struct mqtt_packet ack = {\n                    .header = (union mqtt_header){\n                        .byte = c->session->i_msgs[i].qos == EXACTLY_ONCE\n                                    ? PUBREC_B\n                                    : PUBACK_B}};\n                mqtt_ack(&ack, i);\n                // Set DUP flag to 1\n                mqtt_set_dup(&ack);\n                size = mqtt_size(&ack, NULL);\n                // Serialize the packet and send it out again\n                unsigned char *ack_buf = client_wbuf_reserve(c, size);\n                if (ack_buf) {\n                    mqtt_pack(&ack, ack_buf);\n                    enqueue_event_write(c);\n                }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Introduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe, which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`, and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully instead of silently dropping replies.",
                "markdown": "Introduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe, which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`, and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully instead of silently dropping replies."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "d9d8d47f166e3ef6d11aca28b79815590539fe06c14a428d222a43a5f580a2ec"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "cf0e5ede212de1a756cd86f3d71980c3164d030358509e7996d0efd6b5ceb57e",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:12:24 UTC",
              "target": "sol MQTT broker (C, v0.18.5) - src/",
              "endpoint": "MQTT SUBSCRIBE handler (src/handlers.c subscribe_handler, memcpy at line 617)",
              "method": "MQTT SUBSCRIBE (raw TCP)",
              "cwe": "CWE-787",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated network peer can write attacker-chosen bytes of attacker-chosen length beyond a heap allocation inside the broker process, and reliably terminates the service. Demonstrated consequences are heap corruption outside the `wbuf` object (the overflowed bytes were proven to be the attacker's own PUBLISH payload, landing at `wbuf+908` in a 1024-byte buffer) and process death: AddressSanitizer aborts, and the plain optimized build exits with SIGSEGV on the same input. Because the broker is the single broker in the deployment, every connected client loses its session and message flow, giving a trivial denial of service against all tenants. The write primitive also reaches adjacent heap objects and `write_data()` will transmit `towrite - wrote` bytes from `wbuf`, so out-of-bounds read/disclosure of neighbouring heap contents is reachable once the length tracker is corrupted; that read-back and control-flow hijack were not demonstrated and are not scored here.",
              "technical_analysis": "`client_init()` sizes the write buffer with exactly `conf->max_request_size` bytes and only ever resets the write cursor:\n\n```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=395 endLineNumber=399\n    client->wrote   = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);\n    client->towrite = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);\n    if (!client->wbuf)\n        client->wbuf =\n            try_calloc(conf->max_request_size, sizeof(unsigned char));\n```\n\nNo write site compares `c->towrite + len` against that size. The highest-impact sink is the retained-message replay in `subscribe_handler`, which copies a stored, attacker-created PUBLISH into the buffer once per decoded SUBSCRIBE tuple:\n\n```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=613 endLineNumber=619\n        // Retained message? Publish it\n        // TODO move after SUBACK response\n        if (t->retained_msg) {\n            size_t len = alloc_size(t->retained_msg);\n            memcpy(c->wbuf + c->towrite, t->retained_msg, len);\n            c->towrite += len;\n        }\n```\n\n`len` comes from `alloc_size(t->retained_msg)`, i.e. the size of a serialized PUBLISH that the attacker produced via `publish_handler` (`t->retained_msg = try_alloc(mqtt_size(&e->data, NULL)); mqtt_pack(&e->data, t->retained_msg);`), and `topic_store_get_or_put` returns the same `struct topic *` for a repeated topic name, so duplicate tuples replay the same blob repeatedly. The only bound applied to inbound packets is `pktlen <= max_request_size` in `recv_packet`, which constrains a single PUBLISH, not the sum of the replays.\n\nThe same unchecked pattern appears at every other producer of outbound bytes, which is why the defect is a property of the buffer contract rather than of one handler. `set_connack()` even carried the missing check as a TODO:\n\n```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=330 endLineNumber=338\n        // TODO check for write buffer size exceed\n        if (list_size(c->session->outgoing_msgs) > 0) {\n            size_t len = 0;\n            list_foreach(item, c->session->outgoing_msgs)\n            {\n                len = mqtt_size(item->data, NULL);\n                mqtt_pack(item->data, c->wbuf + c->towrite);\n                c->towrite += len;\n            }\n```\n\nand the inflight retransmit timer repacks every unacknowledged message and every pending ack in one pass:\n\n```c title=src/server.c startLineNumber=336 endLineNumber=339\n                size = mqtt_size(c->session->i_msgs[i].packet, NULL);\n                // Serialize the packet and send it out again\n                mqtt_pack(p, c->wbuf + c->towrite);\n                c->towrite += size;\n```\n\nThe ACK branch of that timer (line 354) additionally sized `&ack` but packed `p`, so the reservation and the write disagreed even when a bound was applied - that mismatch is fixed as part of the same site.\n\nFinally, the corrupted `towrite` is consumed by the writer without any clamp, so the effect does not stop at the memcpy: `send_data(&c->conn, c->wbuf + c->wrote, c->towrite - c->wrote);` transmits past the allocation. `git blame` on the retained-replay lines attributes them to the bulk import commit that introduced the broker (`6c4db6f`, \"Import code\"), i.e. this is original code, not a regression.\n\nThree sinks were reproduced end to end, all pre-authentication under the default `allow_anonymous true` posture, and all sharing the single root cause: no producer ever establishes `towrite + len <= max_request_size` before writing.",
              "remediation_steps": "Introduce a single bounded append primitive for the per-client write buffer and route every producer of outbound bytes through it. The helper must compare the requested length against both the configured buffer size and the bytes already pending, advance the write cursor only when the record fits, and otherwise drop the record and log the event so no caller can write past the allocation. The comparison has to be overflow-safe, which means testing `len > limit - used` rather than `used + len > limit`, and it must also handle a cursor that is already beyond the limit. Apply the helper at every site that serialises into the buffer, not only at the retained-message replay: retained replay, session-resume flush, publish fan-out, the inflight retransmit of messages and of acks, and the fixed-size CONNACK, SUBACK, UNSUBACK, PUBACK/PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP and PINGRESP appends. The small fixed-size appends matter because a buffer filled to within a few bytes by a large record is overrun by the acknowledgement that follows it. Because the same buffer is drained by the socket writer using the pending-bytes counter, the helper is also what keeps that send within bounds; no separate clamp is needed once no site can advance the cursor past the limit. The inflight ACK retransmit additionally has to pack the packet whose size was computed, rather than an unrelated packet, otherwise a correctly sized reservation is still overrun by a larger write. Finally, build the acknowledgement header from the recorded QoS instead of leaving the packet header uninitialised, so the retransmit serialises a well-defined packet. Consider raising `max_request_size` awareness in the protocol layer - for example refusing to replay more retained data than can fit in one write cycle and resuming later - so that a legitimate large subscription degrades gracefully instead of silently dropping replies.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest arguments against this finding were checked explicitly. (1) \"It is only an artefact of the small test `max_request_size`\": the identical sequence was reproduced at 1 MB and at the shipped 50 MB value, producing writes of 3010 and 1048587 bytes past the respective buffers. (2) \"It only happens because AddressSanitizer changes timing or allocation layout\": the plain optimized build, with no sanitizer, dies with SIGSEGV on the same input while the one-tuple control on the same binary stays alive, and the gdb run shows the overflowing bytes are the attacker payload. (3) \"Some upstream control must bound `towrite`\": every producer of outbound bytes was enumerated (`grep` over `mqtt_pack`, `mqtt_pack_mono`, `memcpy` into `wbuf`); before the fix not one of the fourteen sites compared `towrite` with `max_request_size`, and the only inbound limit (`pktlen <= max_request_size` in `recv_packet`) constrains a single packet rather than the accumulated replays. (4) \"The retained payload cannot be that large\": the retained blob is a serialized copy of an attacker PUBLISH, which is itself limited only by `max_request_size`, and duplicate SUBSCRIBE tuples replay the same blob once per tuple, so the sum is unbounded by any single-packet check. (5) \"Dropping records on overflow could hide a real message loss bug\": the drop is logged and only affects the connection that filled its own buffer; the smoke test and the persistent-session regression checks pass unchanged. Not demonstrated, and therefore not scored: conversion of the write primitive into control-flow hijack, and reading back adjacent heap through the oversized `towrite`. No WAF, filter, or validation stands between the peer and these write sites.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that the out-of-bounds write reaches an object used later for dispatch - for example reclaiming the adjacent chunk with attacker data so the overwritten bytes land in a function pointer or `struct ref` free callback - would raise this from high to critical by adding control-flow impact to the existing crash; conversely, evidence that the deployment exposes the broker only over a UNIX socket or behind a peer-authenticated proxy that no untrusted client can reach would lower it to medium.",
              "fix_verification": "All gates were executed on a copy of the audited tree; the read-only target tree was not modified.\n\n**Gate 1 - applicability.** Every `fix_before` block was extracted from the pristine file and every `fix_after` from the patched copy by a script, then all pairs were replayed onto the pristine files and the result compared byte-for-byte with the patched tree:\n\n```\n$ python3 build_locations.py\nRECONSTRUCTION OK  src/server.h  (1 locations)\nRECONSTRUCTION OK  src/server.c  (4 locations)\nRECONSTRUCTION OK  src/handlers.c  (12 locations)\n```\n\nThe patched tree compiles with the project's own production flags (`DEBUG=0` forces `-O3 -Wall -Werror -pedantic`) for both variants:\n\n```\n$ cmake -S . -B build-asan -DDEBUG=0 -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\\n      -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=\"-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fno-sanitize-recover=all\" \\\n      -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE=\"-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fno-sanitize-recover=all\"\n$ cmake --build build-asan --target sol -j\n[100%] Built target sol\n$ cmake -S . -B build-dev -DDEBUG=0 ... -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=\"-O2 -g\"\n$ cmake --build build-dev --target sol -j\n[100%] Built target sol\n```\n\n**Gate 2 - security closure.** Each PoC was re-run against the patched ASan build, then the sequence repeated on the plain build:\n\n```\n$ POC_BIN=build-asan/sol python3 poc_wbuf_overflow.py            # retained replay, 1024 B\nbroker alive after subscribe: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====\nWrite buffer full for wbuf-sub, dropping 910 bytes (910/1024)\n\n$ POC_MAXREQ=1048576 POC_PAYLOAD=3000 POC_NTOPICS=400 POC_MAXMEM=512MB POC_BIN=build-asan/sol \\\n      python3 poc_wbuf_overflow.py\nbroker alive after subscribe: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====          (52 drops logged)\n\n$ POC_MAXREQ=52428800 POC_PAYLOAD=1048576 POC_NTOPICS=52 POC_MAXMEM=2GB POC_BIN=build-asan/sol \\\n      python3 poc_wbuf_overflow.py\nWrite buffer full for wbuf-sub, dropping 1048587 bytes (51380763/52428800)\nbroker alive after subscribe: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====\n\n$ POC_BIN=build-asan/sol python3 poc_wbuf_resume.py\nresume CONNACK: 0100 session_present= 1\nbroker alive after resume: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====          (drops logged at 614/1024)\n\n$ POC_WAIT=32 POC_BIN=build-asan/sol python3 poc_wbuf_inflight.py\ndelivered 6 unacked QoS1 messages of 600 bytes\nbroker alive after 32s retransmit window: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====          (122 drops logged)\n\n$ POC_BIN=build-dev/sol POC_NTOPICS=6 POC_PROVOKE=1 python3 poc_wbuf_overflow.py\nbroker alive after subscribe: True\nbroker alive after provoke: True\n===== NO ASAN REPORT =====          (was exit status -11 before the fix)\n```\n\nThe source-to-sink trace through the patched code: `subscribe_handler` calls `client_wbuf_reserve(c, len)`, which returns `NULL` when `len > max_request_size - towrite`, so the `memcpy` (and every other serialisation) is skipped rather than executed, and `towrite` never exceeds `max_request_size`; `write_data()` therefore sends at most the buffer contents. No path into the buffer bypasses the helper - a `grep` for `wbuf + ` and `towrite +=` in `src/` now matches only the helper's own return statement and the writer's read of the buffer.\n\n**Gate 3 - bypass review.** The diff was re-read for sibling sinks and alternate input classes. (a) Duplicate SUBSCRIBE tuples, the strongest variant because it needs only one retained message: on the pristine build it crashes with `WRITE of size 910 ... handlers.c:617`, and on the patched build the same sequence leaves the broker alive with 2 drops logged. (b) Several unacked QoS1 messages: covered by the inflight retransmit run above. (c) Session resume: covered above. (d) Near-exact-fit sizes (retained blobs of 1000, 1010 and 1015 bytes) followed by the SUBACK append: the patched broker survives all three; this is why the fixed-size acknowledgement sites were converted too, since the pristine code overruns by the ack length once a large record leaves fewer bytes than the ack needs. (e) Multiple QoS levels and a QoS 0 fan-out were exercised through the smoke test and the refcount regression script. The review additionally found that bounding the ACK branch of `inflight_msg_check` was not sufficient on its own: that branch sized `&ack` but packed `p`, so after adding the helper it still wrote 600 bytes into a 4-byte reservation and ASan fired at `server.c` in the first iteration of this verification. The location entry for that site therefore also makes the packed object match the reserved size; with that change the timer run is clean.\n\n**Gate 4 - preserved behaviour.** The harness smoke test passes unchanged (7/7) and a persistent-session regression script passes 8/8:\n\n```\n$ python3 test_smoke.py --port 18905\n[PASS] anonymous CONNECT accepted (CONNACK rc=0)\n[PASS] SUBSCRIBE -> SUBACK (granted, not 0x80)\n[PASS] second connection CONNECT accepted\n[PASS] PUBLISH delivered to subscriber\n[PASS] PINGREQ -> PINGRESP\n[PASS] DISCONNECT accepted (sockets closed cleanly)\n[PASS] broker still accepting connections after the run\nRESULT: PASS  (7/7 checks)\n\n$ python3 verify_refcount.py 18909\n[PASS] T1 persistent session resumes  -- 0100\n[PASS] T1 reused session still usable\n[PASS] T2 clean session is not resumed  -- 0000\n[PASS] T3 borrower told session_present  -- 0100\n[PASS] T3 borrower SUBACK after owner left  -- 000700\n[PASS] T3 borrower still receives publishes\n[PASS] T3 later connection starts a fresh session  -- 0000\n[PASS] broker still serving\nRESULT: PASS\n```\n\nNo ASan report was produced during either run. The only behavioural change is that a connection which cannot fit a record loses that record with a logged `Write buffer full` line instead of corrupting the heap; the SUBACK is dropped in the pathological case where the retained replay has already filled the buffer, which is a deliberate trade for memory safety.\n\n**Gate 5 - repository checks.** The upstream unit-test target builds and passes in both variants:\n\n```\n$ cmake --build build-dev --target sol_test -j\ntests run: 13 tests passed: 13\n$ cmake --build build-asan --target sol_test -j\ntests run: 13 tests passed: 13\n```\n\n**Gaps.** No dedicated upstream test covers the write buffer, so the closure evidence rests on the PoC runs plus the smoke and refcount scripts rather than on a repository test that fails without the fix. The inflight ACK path was exercised only through the 20-second timer PoC, not with a QoS 2 PUBREL/PUBCOMP handshake, because the broker's QoS 2 state machine has independent defects outside this finding's scope.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build the broker with AddressSanitizer and start it on 127.0.0.1 with `max_request_size 1024`, `allow_anonymous true`.\n2. Open a first connection and publish two retained PUBLISH packets (`a/000`, `a/001`) with a 900-byte payload; each stored retained blob serializes to 910 bytes.\n3. Open a second connection and send one SUBSCRIBE packet carrying both topic tuples at QoS 0.\n4. The replay loop copies 910 bytes at offset 0 and then 910 bytes at offset 910 into a 1024-byte heap buffer: AddressSanitizer reports a `heap-buffer-overflow` WRITE of size 910 immediately after the 1032-byte region (1024 bytes plus the allocator header) at `src/handlers.c:617`, and the process aborts.\n5. Repeat with one tuple only (910 bytes, fits): the broker stays alive and no sanitizer report is produced, confirming the trigger is the unbounded append rather than the retained-message feature.\n6. Repeat against the plain optimized build with six tuples: the broker exits with SIGSEGV (status -11) instead of an ASan abort, confirming a production-build crash.\n7. Scale the same sequence to `max_request_size` 1 MB (400 retained messages of 3000 bytes, 400 tuples) and to the shipped 50 MB (52 retained messages of 1 MiB) to confirm the defect is not an artefact of the small test configuration.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-416",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pre-authentication use-after-free of a shared MQTT session on ClientId reuse\n\nThe handler that lets a second connection resume an existing MQTT session by ClientId does not take a reference on that session, while the connection that created it releases the only reference when it disconnects. The session object is therefore freed while the second connection is still connected and still using it, and that connection then reads and writes freed heap memory. Any unauthenticated peer can set this up with two connections and one disconnect."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/handlers.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 401,
                  "endLine": 405,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (cc->session && c->bits.clean_session == true)\n        // Clean session true, we have to clean old session, if any\n        HASH_DEL(server.sessions, cc->session);\n    else if (cc->session)\n        session_present = 1;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Session reuse: take a reference for the resuming connection and release the map reference when the old session is discarded"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/handlers.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 417,
                  "endLine": 418,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "cc->session = client_session_alloc(cc->client_id);\n        INCREF(cc->session, struct client_session);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Session creation: one reference for the connection plus one for the sessions map entry"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/server.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 430,
                  "endLine": 443,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (client->clean_session == true) {\n        if (client->session) {\n            topic_store_remove_wildcard(server.store, client->client_id);\n            list_foreach(item, client->session->subscriptions)\n            {\n                topic_del_subscriber(item->data, client);\n            }\n            HASH_DEL(server.sessions, client->session);\n            DECREF(client->session, struct client_session);\n        }\n        if (client->connected == true)\n            HASH_DEL(server.clients_map, client);\n        memorypool_free(server.pool, client);\n    }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "client_deactivate(): release the connection's reference on every path, the map reference only on clean_session"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "MQTT CONNECT handler (src/handlers.c connect_handler, session lookup at lines 400-405)",
