          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/handlers.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 128,
                        "endLine": 134
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "static inline void inflight_msg_init(struct inflight_msg *imsg,\n                                     struct mqtt_packet *p)\n{\n    imsg->seen   = time(NULL);\n    imsg->packet = p;\n    imsg->qos    = p->header.bits.qos;\n}\n\n/*\n * Return the in-flight slot owned by the given packet identifier, or NULL when\n * the client has no session yet or the identifier was never allocated (or was\n * already acknowledged). A peer may ACK any 16 bit identifier it likes, so the\n * slot has to be validated before it is dereferenced and DECREF'd.\n */\nstatic struct inflight_msg *inflight_msg_get(struct client *c, unsigned pkt_id)\n{\n    if (c->session == NULL || pkt_id >= (unsigned)MAX_INFLIGHT_MSGS)\n        return NULL;\n    if (c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id].packet == NULL)\n        return NULL;\n    return &c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id];\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/handlers.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 785,
                        "endLine": 801
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "static int puback_handler(struct io_event *e)\n{\n    struct client *c = e->client;\n    unsigned pkt_id  = e->data.ack.pkt_id;\n    struct inflight_msg *imsg = NULL;\n\n    log_debug(\"Received PUBACK from %s (m%u)\", c->client_id, pkt_id);\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    imsg = inflight_msg_get(c, pkt_id);\n    if (imsg == NULL) {\n        /*\n         * Identifier we never allocated, or one already acknowledged by a\n         * previous ACK/LWT replay: it owns no in-flight packet, so there is\n         * nothing to release and the duplicate must be ignored\n         */\n        log_debug(\"Ignoring PUBACK from %s for unknown packet id %u\",\n                  c->client_id, pkt_id);\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n        return NOREPLY;\n    }\n    inflight_msg_clear(imsg);\n    c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id].packet = NULL;\n    c->session->i_acks[pkt_id]        = -1;\n    if (c->session->inflights > 0)\n        --c->session->inflights;\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    return NOREPLY;\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/handlers.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 839,
                        "endLine": 855
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "static int pubcomp_handler(struct io_event *e)\n{\n    struct client *c = e->client;\n    unsigned pkt_id  = e->data.ack.pkt_id;\n    struct inflight_msg *imsg = NULL;\n\n    log_debug(\"Received PUBCOMP from %s (m%u)\", c->client_id, pkt_id);\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    imsg = inflight_msg_get(c, pkt_id);\n    if (imsg == NULL) {\n        /* Not an identifier we allocated, ignore the duplicate/spurious ack */\n        log_debug(\"Ignoring PUBCOMP from %s for unknown packet id %u\",\n                  c->client_id, pkt_id);\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n        return NOREPLY;\n    }\n    c->session->i_acks[pkt_id] = -1;\n    inflight_msg_clear(imsg);\n    c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id].packet = NULL;\n    if (c->session->inflights > 0)\n        --c->session->inflights;\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    return NOREPLY;\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Treat every acknowledgement as untrusted input and validate ownership before touching the in-flight table. Add a single helper that returns the in-flight slot for a packet identifier only when the client actually has a session, the identifier is inside the table, and the slot holds a live packet; have the PUBACK and PUBCOMP handlers call it under the client mutex and, when it returns nothing, log and ignore the acknowledgement instead of dereferencing and decrementing it. Decrement the in-flight counter only when a live slot was released, so the counter cannot wrap. Apply the same ownership rule to any future handler that resolves a client-supplied packet identifier into session state (for example PUBREC/PUBREL bookkeeping), and consider rejecting ACK packets outright on connections that have not completed CONNECT, so session state is never reached before authentication.",
                "markdown": "Treat every acknowledgement as untrusted input and validate ownership before touching the in-flight table. Add a single helper that returns the in-flight slot for a packet identifier only when the client actually has a session, the identifier is inside the table, and the slot holds a live packet; have the PUBACK and PUBCOMP handlers call it under the client mutex and, when it returns nothing, log and ignore the acknowledgement instead of dereferencing and decrementing it. Decrement the in-flight counter only when a live slot was released, so the counter cannot wrap. Apply the same ownership rule to any future handler that resolves a client-supplied packet identifier into session state (for example PUBREC/PUBREL bookkeeping), and consider rejecting ACK packets outright on connections that have not completed CONNECT, so session state is never reached before authentication."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "c42aa73965b16c846779df9f7d419d22e2a02af55f819186de8f50461363bf36"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "36f063a0288884691893b934970a773baa2a70d146ee829607fe037bf12dfff6",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:14:55 UTC",
              "target": "sol MQTT v3.1.1 broker (C source snapshot, `src/`)",
              "endpoint": "MQTT TCP listener — PUBACK (0x40) / PUBCOMP (0x70) handlers, `inflight_msg_clear` / `DECREF`",
              "method": "MQTT (raw TCP)",
              "cwe": "CWE-476",
              "impact": "**Unauthenticated, zero-interaction availability loss for the whole broker process.** Four bytes (`40 02 00 01` or `70 02 00 01`) on a fresh TCP connection — no CONNECT, no credentials — terminate the listener with SIGSEGV. Every connected client is dropped and all in-memory session, in-flight and retained-message state is lost until an external supervisor restarts the process.\n\nThe same crash is also reachable **without any attacker intent**: after a legitimate QoS 1 acknowledgement, a client that retransmits that PUBACK (normal retry behaviour after a reconnect or timeout) dereferences the NULL slot the first ACK just cleared, so ordinary client traffic can take the broker down. Because a single broker process serves all clients, this is a denial of service against the whole deployment, not just one session.\n\nNo confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed: the faulting pointer is a fixed NULL-derived address, so no attacker-controlled write or read was demonstrated (`C:N` / `I:N` / `A:H`).",
              "technical_analysis": "## Root cause\n\n`puback_handler` and `pubcomp_handler` take the 16-bit packet identifier straight out of the decoded packet, index the per-session in-flight table with it, and hand the resulting slot to `inflight_msg_clear()`:\n\n```c title=src/handlers.c startLineNumber=785 endLineNumber=801\nstatic int puback_handler(struct io_event *e)\n{\n    struct client *c = e->client;\n    unsigned pkt_id  = e->data.ack.pkt_id;\n    log_debug(\"Received PUBACK from %s (m%u)\", c->client_id, pkt_id);\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    inflight_msg_clear(&c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id]);\n    c->session->i_msgs[pkt_id].packet = NULL;\n    c->session->i_acks[pkt_id]        = -1;\n    --c->session->inflights;\n#if THREADSNR > 0\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&c->mutex);\n#endif\n    return NOREPLY;\n}\n```\n\n(`pubcomp_handler`, `src/handlers.c:839-855`, is the same shape with the `i_acks` write first.)\n\n`inflight_msg_clear` is a bare reference-count decrement with no NULL check:\n\n```c title=src/sol_internal.h startLineNumber=389 endLineNumber=389\n#define inflight_msg_clear(msg) DECREF((msg)->packet, struct mqtt_packet)\n```\n\n```c title=src/ref.h startLineNumber=50 endLineNumber=54\nstatic inline void ref_dec(const struct ref *ref)\n{\n    if (--((struct ref *)ref)->count == 0)\n        ref->free(ref);\n}\n```\n\nTwo facts turn that into a remote crash:\n\n- **`c->session` can be NULL.** `client_init` sets `client->session = NULL` (`src/server.c:402`) and nothing on the dispatch path checks `c->connected` before running a handler, so an ACK sent as the very first packet dereferences `NULL + offsetof(i_msgs)`.\n- **`i_msgs` is zero-filled.** `session_init` allocates it with `try_calloc(MAX_INFLIGHT_MSGS, sizeof(struct inflight_msg))` (`src/handlers.c:109-110`), so `i_msgs[id].packet == NULL` for every identifier the broker never allocated. `DECREF(NULL, struct mqtt_packet)` becomes `ref_dec` on a pointer derived from NULL, and the `--count` read-modify-write faults near address zero.\n\nPacket identifiers are entirely client-chosen and the broker is not required to have allocated any given one, so this is reachable with `40 02 00 01` (PUBACK, id 1) or `70 02 00 01` (PUBCOMP, id 1). `0xFFFF` crashes too, confirming the index is in range and the defect is the unguarded NULL rather than an out-of-bounds access.\n\n## Why this also happens without an attacker\n\nAfter a legitimate PUBACK clears `i_msgs[mid].packet` and sets it to NULL, a client that retransmits the same PUBACK — normal QoS 1 behaviour after a reconnect or a timeout — runs the same code again with the slot now empty and kills the broker. The broker therefore dies on its own intended traffic pattern, not only on crafted input.\n\n## Related defect examined and ruled out\n\nThe `--c->session->inflights` decrement on an `atomic_ushort` is unguarded and would wrap to 65535, permanently satisfying `has_inflight()`. It is **not** independently reachable: both decrement sites are preceded by `inflight_msg_clear`, which crashes first when the slot is empty, and when the slot is non-NULL the only writer (`publish_message`, `src/handlers.c:216-218`) increments the counter for the same identifier, so the counter is at least 1. Reusing an identifier that is still in flight would require `next_free_mid` to wrap after ~65534 allocations. It is recorded here only as the latent side effect that the fix removes, not as a separate vulnerability.",
              "remediation_steps": "Treat every acknowledgement as untrusted input and validate ownership before touching the in-flight table. Add a single helper that returns the in-flight slot for a packet identifier only when the client actually has a session, the identifier is inside the table, and the slot holds a live packet; have the PUBACK and PUBCOMP handlers call it under the client mutex and, when it returns nothing, log and ignore the acknowledgement instead of dereferencing and decrementing it. Decrement the in-flight counter only when a live slot was released, so the counter cannot wrap. Apply the same ownership rule to any future handler that resolves a client-supplied packet identifier into session state (for example PUBREC/PUBREL bookkeeping), and consider rejecting ACK packets outright on connections that have not completed CONNECT, so session state is never reached before authentication.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest counterargument is that an ACK for a packet identifier the broker never allocated is malformed client behaviour, so a peer \"should not\" send it. That does not hold up: the broker must survive arbitrary protocol input from an unauthenticated peer, packet identifiers are entirely client-chosen, and the same four bytes are harmless when the identifier happens to be allocated — so the crash is decided purely by whether the broker has allocated that id, not by anything the client can know. Worse, the duplicate case needs no malice at all: a client retransmitting a PUBACK for a mid it already acknowledged (normal QoS 1 behaviour after a lost connection or retry) kills the broker, which makes this reachable from the broker's own intended traffic pattern.\n\nA second candidate — the unguarded `--c->session->inflights` decrement on an `atomic_ushort`, which would wrap to 65535 — was examined and ruled out as an independent defect: both decrement sites are preceded by `inflight_msg_clear`, which crashes first whenever the slot is empty, and when the slot is non-NULL the only writer (`publish_message`) increments the counter next to the same mid, so no reachable sequence decrements below zero. It is reported here only as the side effect the fix removes, not as a separate vulnerability.\n\nNo auth bypass, write primitive, or information leak was demonstrated: the dereferenced address is a fixed small offset derived from NULL (`0x78`/`0x80` region), not attacker-influenced.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced on the sanitizer and plain optimized builds for PUBACK and PUBCOMP, with and without a CONNECT, for packet ids 1 and 0xFFFF, plus an in-band negative control and an operator-free duplicate-ACK path; the rebuilt patch keeps the legitimate QoS1/QoS2 acks working.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the broker runs as a single unsupervised instance with no restart policy would raise this from a transient crash to a sustained outage; automated sub-second restart would lower the practical impact. Evidence that the NULL-derived address can be steered (for example by influencing the session pointer) would turn it into a write primitive and add integrity impact.",
              "fix_verification": "All work was done in a private copy of the tree; the reported tree was not modified. Both variants were rebuilt from scratch with the project's production flags (`DEBUG=0` implies `-O3 -Wall -Werror -pedantic -std=c11`) and compiled warning-free.\n\n**Gate 1 — security closure (executed).** Rebuilt `build-asan` (`-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all`) and `build-dev` (`-O2 -g`), then re-ran every crash case:\n\n```\npatched dev  : PUBACK  id=1     after CONNECT   reply <no bytes>   broker SURVIVED\npatched dev  : PUBCOMP id=1     after CONNECT   reply <no bytes>   broker SURVIVED\npatched dev  : PUBACK  id=1     no CONNECT      reply <no bytes>   broker SURVIVED\npatched dev  : PUBACK  id=0xffff                reply <no bytes>   broker SURVIVED\npatched dev  : PUBCOMP id=0xffff                reply <no bytes>   broker SURVIVED\npatched ASan : PUBACK / PUBCOMP / PUBACK-without-CONNECT  -> broker SURVIVED, no sanitizer report\n```\n\nThe patched control flow returns before any dereference: `inflight_msg_get()` rejects `c->session == NULL` and `i_msgs[pkt_id].packet == NULL`, so `inflight_msg_clear()` (and therefore `ref_dec`) is unreachable for an unowned identifier. The connection is not closed on this path; the spurious ack is simply ignored, which is the behaviour a broker should have.\n\n**Gate 2 — bypass review (executed).** Alternates exercised against the patched build: PUBACK and PUBCOMP with id 1 and id 0xFFFF; with no CONNECT at all; and the duplicate-after-legal-ack sequence that previously crashed (`dup-puback` flow now ends `t=+0.3s poll=None … broker SURVIVED` on both dev and ASan). `pubrec_handler` was checked as a sibling: it only writes `i_acks[pkt_id]` inside the 65536-entry array and packs a PUBREL, so it does not touch the packet pointer and needs no change; `pubrel_handler` likewise only packs. `session_free` already NULL-checks before DECREF, so teardown of a session with holes is unaffected. The `inflights` counter is now decremented only on the live path, so the latent unsigned wrap disappears as a side effect (it was never independently reachable).\n\n**Gate 3 — preserved behaviour (executed).** A dedicated flow check on the patched dev build:\n\n```\nQoS1 publisher ack : 40 00 07\nQoS1 subscriber    : PUBLISH mid=1 -> PUBACK accepted -> PINGREQ d0\nQoS2 publisher     : PUBREC 50 00 05 -> (PUBREL sent) -> PUBCOMP 70 00 05\nQoS2 subscriber    : PUBLISH mid=1 -> PUBREC -> PUBREL 62 00 01 -> PUBCOMP accepted -> PINGREQ d0\nbroker after the flows: ALIVE, RESULT: PASS\n```\n\nThat covers both the PUBACK path and the PUBCOMP path with a genuinely allocated identifier — the subscriber's PUBCOMP is the exact call site the fix guards. The end-to-end smoke suite also passes 7/7 on both patched builds. One gap, verified as pre-existing rather than a regression of this change: UNSUBSCRIBE kills the unpatched tree as well (the decoded packet is destroyed twice, in `unsubscribe_handler` and in `process_message`), so it cannot be used as an extra positive control; both trees behave identically.\n\n**Gate 4 — repository checks (executed).** `cmake --build build-dev --target sol_test` ran the upstream unit suite: `tests run: 13 tests passed: 13`; both `sol` targets built with `-Werror` and no diagnostics. Not run: `tests/integration/` (requires an external MQTT client library unavailable here) — recorded as a gap, not claimed as passing.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Start the `sol` broker on a loopback port from the ASan/UBSan build (`build-asan/sol`) or the plain optimized build (`build-dev/sol`).\n2. Open a TCP connection and send the CONNECT packet (`10 19 00 04 4d 51 54 54 04 02 00 3c 00 0d 70 6c 76 2d 61 63 6b 2d 70 72 6f 62 65`); the broker answers `20 00 00`.\n3. Send the four bytes `40 02 00 01` (PUBACK, Remaining Length 2, packet identifier 1, which the broker never allocated).\n4. Observe the connection close with no reply, and the broker process terminate: the sanitizer build reports `member access within null pointer of type 'struct mqtt_packet'` at the `inflight_msg_clear` call in `puback_handler` (`src/handlers.c:793`); the plain build dies with signal 11.\n5. Repeat with `70 02 00 01` (PUBCOMP) — same result at `src/handlers.c:848`. Repeat with `40 02 ff ff` / `70 02 ff ff` — same, showing the identifier is in range and the problem is the NULL slot.\n6. Repeat step 3 without any CONNECT: the session pointer itself is NULL and the crash is identical, so no credentials or prior handshake are needed.\n7. Control: perform a real QoS 1 round trip (SUBSCRIBE at QoS 1, publish from a second connection, receive the QoS 1 PUBLISH with its mid, then PUBACK that mid) — the same four bytes are accepted, PINGREQ afterwards is answered `d0`, and the broker stays alive.\n8. Duplicate case: after the legal PUBACK in step 7, send the same `40 02 00 01` again — the broker terminates with signal 11, with no attacker input beyond the client retrying its own acknowledgement.\n9. Apply the patch, rebuild, and repeat steps 3, 5, 6 and 8: no crash, the spurious acks are ignored, and the legitimate round trip in step 7 still works.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-191",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Non-terminating SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE tuple loops: unsigned underflow lets one 7-byte pre-auth packet crash or exhaust the broker\n\nBoth the SUBSCRIBE and the UNSUBSCRIBE decoders walk their payload in a loop whose counter is the sender-declared packet length, and they subtract the declared topic length from it before checking that those bytes are actually present. Declaring a topic longer than the packet wraps the counter to about 2^64, so the loop never terminates: it keeps reallocating and allocating memory and keeps copying from beyond the end of the connection's read buffer. A single unauthenticated 7-byte packet kills the shipped build with SIGSEGV in about 0.2 s; with a larger read buffer it instead grows resident memory by roughly 900 MB in half a second while pinning a worker thread at full CPU, and the process no longer honours SIGTERM."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
