        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/controlled-reallocation-of-freed-session-memory-uaf-attacker-bytes",
          "ruleIndex": 35,
          "kind": "open",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Controlled reallocation of freed session memory (UAF -> attacker bytes) — Requires further review: Reuse of the freed client_session chunk with attacker-controlled PUBLISH payload (session UAF exploitation)\n\nSize classes match (sizeof(client_session)=320, try_alloc header 8 -> 328-byte malloc request; a retained PUBLISH packed to 320 bytes issues the same 328-byte request), but /workspace/poc/poc_session_reuse.sh (gdb, non-ASan build) still shows the original \"victim\" bytes when subscriber_new copies session->session_id, i.e. the freed chunk was not reclaimed. The free and the later allocation land on different event-loop threads (THREADSNR 2 + main loop all epoll one listener), so the chunk stays in another thread's tcache; ASan quarantine blocks the experiment there. Not demonstrated; would need thread placement control or tcache-draining grooming."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "Reuse of the freed client_session chunk with attacker-controlled PUBLISH payload (session UAF exploitation)"
